sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch10-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The heart and stomach develop from simple tubes by dilatation and twisting; their originally circular and longitudinal fibres remain geodetic on the distorted surface, new geodetic systems (such as the oblique stomach fibres) appear on new pouches, and the heart contracts effectively only because its fibres run the shortest course.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Heart and stomach fibres stay geodetic after the twisting of the primitive tube";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter X, “A Parenthetic Note on Geodetics”; topic “Geodetics of heart and stomach”. Scope: vertebrate (especially mammalian) heart and stomach musculature"
}
